===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# [[doublespeak|Doublespeak]].
# [[Lies]], especially in a formal [[political]] statement.
# {{senseid|en|comedy}} {{lb|en|comedy}} Speech which combines [[English]] and English-sounding [[gibberish]] for [[humorous]] effect.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Rick DesRochers|title=The Comic Offense from Vaudeville to Contemporary Comedy|publisher=Bloomsbury|isbn=9781441161932|page=15|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8r_GAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA15&dq=%22double+talk%22+caesar&hl=&cd=1&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22double%20talk%22%20caesar&f=false|text=[Sid] Caesar's vaudeville format included burlesques—in the nineteenth-century meaning of parody—featuring silent-film and film noir send-ups; Italian opera produced in Caesar's ad-libbed, faux-Italian '''double-talk''';{{nb...}}}}
# A simple [[phonetic]] [[code]] with a regular [[infix]] that makes meaningful speech sound [[unintelligible]].
# {{lb|en|telephony}} A situation when two people talk at the same time, causing overlapping audio signals.

